ry epilepsy, providing an alternative treatment option when conventional medications fail. This dietary approach has shown significant efficacy in reducing the frequency and severity of seizures, particularly in children and adolescents. The diet's mechanism involves a high-fat, low-carbohydrate intake that alters the brain's energy metabolism, making it a valuable therapeutic strategy for individuals suffering from intractable epilepsy. [Data: Entities (6570, 11240, 5463); Relationships (15831, 15820, 15866)]\n\n## Types of seizures targeted by the ketogenic diet\n\nThe ketogenic diet is effective in managing various types of seizures, including tonic-clonic, absence, and atonic seizures. These seizures can significantly impact the quality of life for patients, and the diet aims to reduce their occurrence. Research indicates that the diet can be particularly beneficial for patients with conditions like Lennox-Gastaut syndrome and tuberous sclerosis, which are associated with multiple seizure types. [Data: Entities (4052, 11234, 11232, 11235, 11215); Relationships (15816, 15821, 15822)]\n\n## Potential complications of the ketogenic diet\n\nWhile the ketogenic diet can be effective, it is also associated with several potential complications, including gastrointestinal issues, metabolic acidosis, and cardiac complications. Patients may experience side effects such as diarrhea, nausea, and vomiting, particularly during the initial phase of the diet. Long-term adherence to the diet can lead to chronic acidosis, which increases the risk of bone demineralization and other health issues. Monitoring and management of these complications are crucial for patient safety. [Data: Entities (10087, 11275, 11274); Relationships (4209, 15865, 15847)]\n\n## Importance of adherence to the ketogenic diet\n\nAdherence to the ketogenic diet is a critical factor influencing its effectiveness in seizure management. Studies have shown that good adherence correlates with better seizure control and overall health outcomes. Factors affecting adherence include the diet's restrictive nature, potential side effects, and the need for ongoing monitoring. Support from healthcare providers and family members can enhance adherence and improve treatment outcomes for patients. [Data: Entities (11292, 11287); Relationships (15868, 15866)]\n\n## Research and evidence supporting the ketogenic diet\n\nNumerous studies and systematic reviews, including Cochrane reviews, have evaluated the efficacy of the ketogenic diet in treating epilepsy. These studies provide robust evidence supporting the diet's role in reducing seizure frequency and improving the quality of life for patients with refractory epilepsy. Ongoing research continues to explore the long-term effects and safety of the diet, contributing to the growing body of literature on dietary interventions for epilepsy. [Data: Entities (11242, 11241); Relationships (15834, 15866)]\"\r\n\n\n-----Entities-----\nhuman_readable_id,title,description,degree\r\n1013,EPILEPSIA,\"Epilepsia is a prominent medical journal dedicated to publishing research related to epilepsy and seizure disorders. It focuses on various aspects of epilepsy, including treatment approaches and dietary interventions, notably the ketogenic diet, which has been historically significant in managing the condition. The journal serves as a peer-reviewed platform for studies that explore the neurological manifestations of epilepsy, including its occurrence in specific patient populations, such as those with MELAS (Mitochondrial Encephalomyopathy, Lactic Acidosis, and Stroke-like episodes), where epilepsy contributes to their overall health challenges.\n\nEpilepsy itself is a neurological disorder characterized by recurrent seizures, which can significantly impact treatment strategies for affected individuals, including those with conditions like oropharyngeal dysphagia. The condition is recognized in both English and Spanish literature, emphasizing its gl